  "query_preview": "..The large scale pattern has a trough to our west \nand high pressure aloft centered over northeast Mexico and Texas \nthrough Thursday. A system will move across the northern tier of \nstates Friday and Saturday, pushing the upper high to the southeast \nover the Gulf. A low pressure system and associated trough will then \nmove across California and the Great Basin early next week.\n\nThe flow across southeast Arizona between the trough to the west and \nthe upper high to the east remains southerly through Thursday. PWATs \nof 1.0 to 1.25 inches across the forecast area today/tonight will \nlower to near 1.0 inch Thursday. There will be a chance of showers \nand thunderstorms from Sells eastward today, and mainly from Tucson \neastward Thursday. A disturbance tracking northward through central \nSonora will move into southeast Arizona late this afternoon/early \nevening, helping to enhance shower and thunderstorm development. The \nhigh resolution models mainly keep shower activity across the \neastern counties today, with the main bulk of thunderstorm \ndevelopment through the evening hours tonight roughly in an area \nbounded from Sierra Vista to Oracle westward to Sells. The stronger \nthunderstorms through Thursday will be capable of producing heavy \nrainfall and an isolated threat of flash flooding.  \n\nDrier air begins to overspread southeast Arizona from the west on \nFriday, as the flows turns southwest/west in response to a system \ntracking east across the northern tier of states. There will still \nbe a slight chance of showers and thunderstorms on Friday across far \neastern areas. However by Saturday, PWATs lower down to around 0.75 \ninches and convection should be shunted to our east across New \nMexico and southward into Sonora Mexico. \n  \nSunday into early next week, the combination of an upper low/trough \nmoving into the West Coast and Hurricane Polo moving northward along \nthe Mexico coast will help spread moisture back across southeast \nArizona from the south. This moisture begins to move back into the \narea on Sunday for a slight chance of showers and thunderstorms, \nwith deeper moisture moving in Monday and Tuesday and a chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms across most of southeast Arizona. The \ntrack of Hurricane Polo remains uncertain once it reaches as far \nnorth as Cabo. There is a near equal split in the models and \nensemble members turning the system west as those curving it east \ninto southern/central Sonora. The exact track will remain highly \ndependent upon the timing/depth of the trough and position of Polo \nwhen the trough moves into the west coast. \n\nBehind the trough on Wednesday of next week there will be a slight \nchance of showers and thunderstorms. This marks the official end to \nthe Monsoon Season (September 30). The moisture profile late next \nweek reflects the end of the monsoon, as high pressure aloft builds \nover the eastern Pacific and Baja Peninsula and southeast Arizona \ndries out significantly.  \n\nOverall, high temperatures will be several degrees below normal \nthrough Thursday, warming to within a couple of degrees of normal \nover the weekend, then lowering back below normal again through \nthe middle of next week.",
